Make "\\" one backslash in the strings of y.
POSIX says that in the strings of y two backslashes stand for one
literal backslash, but sed kept both, so the two strings were out of
step from there on: the source of "y/\\n/xy/" was the three bytes "\",
"\" and "n", which left the "n" without a partner, so "a\n" became
"axn" instead of "axy". Unescape "\\" after the other escapes, which
already keep a "\\" pair together, so "\\n" is a backslash and an "n"
and not a newline.

---
 editors/sed.c |    3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -645,6 +645,9 @@
 		/* \n already parsed, but \delimiter needs unescaping. */
 		parse_escapes(match,   match,   -1, i, i);
 		parse_escapes(replace, replace, -1, i, i);
+		/* "\\" is one backslash */
+		parse_escapes(match,   match,   -1, '\\', '\\');
+		parse_escapes(replace, replace, -1, '\\', '\\');
 
 		sed_cmd->string = xzalloc((strlen(match) + 1) * 2);
 		for (i = 0; match[i] && replace[i]; i++) {
